1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 Here a complexity is added to our computation of taxable income.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 Namely we work with depreciation, recapture and capital gain.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 I think the easiest way to understand is to look at this figure.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 So we have here bottom line, 0.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 We have our first cost B, or our unadjusted basis B as we called it.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 And then we have a book value in year T.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 Now suppose that we are selling an asset, then we have three situations that can happen.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 Either the selling price is higher than our first cost,

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 our selling price is between the first cost and the book value,

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 or our selling price is lower than our book value.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 And these three situations give rise to three different values.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 If our selling price is lower than our book value, we are talking about a capital loss.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:16,000
 If our selling price is somewhere between the first cost and our book value,

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 we are talking about a depreciation recapture, sometimes called an order gain.

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 And in the situation that we are very lucky, that we get more money than we initially paid,

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 we are talking about a capital gain.

17
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 So these are the three values that we have to take into account.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 So that means that our taxable income relation is updated and we add these amounts.

19
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 Of course, we will see what impact it has.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 Obviously in a situation you cannot have at the same time a capital loss and a net capital gain.

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 So we will have just an example.

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 So it might seem complicated, but in fact it's not.

23
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 So we are talking about a laser based system that was installed three years ago for a B value of 150,000.

24
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 And we can sell it now for 180,000.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 Based on a five-year macros recovery, the book value currently is 43,200.

26
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 The gross income for this year is an 800,000 and annual operating expenses are 50,000.

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 So we are asked to determine the taxable income and then the taxes if we have a tax rate of 34%.

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 So if we sell the system now.

29
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 So let's see what we have.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 So we have the situation in here.

31
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 So this is zero.

32
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 Our book value is 43,200.

33
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 And our B value was 150,000.

34
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 And our selling price is 180,000.

35
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:28,000
 So what do we have?

36
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 Well we have a depreciation recapture.

37
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
 And we have a capital gain.

38
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 So that means that we will have to add that to our taxable income formula.

39
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 So we have our 800,000 for our gross income.

40
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 We have our 50,000 for our operating expenses.

41
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 We have our 28,000 for our depreciation.

42
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 And we have our depreciation recapture and capital gain.

43
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 And that gives us a taxable income of 858,000 times our tax rate.

44
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 That gives us 290,720.

45
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 And what you see here.

46
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 Well if you would not have sold the item or the asset now.

47
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 We would have a different taxable income.

48
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 Because we would not have had that depreciation, recovery and capital gain.

49
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 Now an after tax evaluation.

50
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
 Well this is a full slide.

51
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
 But in fact what it's saying is.

52
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 Well just act the same way as you would do.

53
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 As you did with the before taxes.

54
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 The guidelines are still the same.

55
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 For instance present word.

56
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 If it's positive.

57
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 Well it's a viable project.

58
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 If you have more than one.

59
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
 And they are mutually exclusive.

60
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
 Then the one with the highest or the best present word value will win.

61
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 We also still have that equal in terms of present word comparison.

62
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 We still have that equal service requirement.

63
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 Also for the ROAR we still have the same things.

64
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 That the ROAR should be better than the after tax more.

65
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 And also that the best one we need that incremental analysis.

66
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
 So not a lot of new things.

67
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 We can estimate the after tax ROAR.

68
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 And here you see a simple numerical example.

69
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 So we have the values given here.

70
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 For P, for GI minus OE.

71
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 For N, for D, for TE.

72
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 And if we set up the annual word analysis before the present word relationship.

73
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 For our I star, our ROAR.

74
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:22,000
 We get a before tax one of 28.65%.

75
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
 After ROAR we would say well this is this.

76
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
 And we multiply it by times one minus the tax rate.

77
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
 And it's giving us 17.19.

78
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:42,000
 Now if you do the analysis again after tax you would end up with 18.03.

79
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 So estimation is in most cases relatively good.

80
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
 But of course it's not an exact thing.

81
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 An after tax analysis.

82
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
 Example, so we have our B value to an adjusted basis.

83
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 We have a salvage value of zero.

84
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 We have a lifetime of five years.

85
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:10,000
 And per year we have a revenue, we have our operating expenses and we have a depreciation.

86
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:16,000
 And we have an effective tax rate of 0.184.

87
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:26,000
 Before taxes we have our revenue, we have our expenses, we have our base value, we have our salvage value which is zero.

88
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
 So we can compute the cash flow before taxes.

89
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 And we can, based on this cash flow series, we can determine our rate of return.

90
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
 Okay, let's do it for the after taxes.

91
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
 We have our depreciation that comes into play.

92
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 Taxable income.

93
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 And the taxable income is then based on those two values plus of course our depreciation.

94
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
 We have our tax rates and we can compute our cash flow after taxes.

95
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
 If we have a look at this cash flow series we can again determine the ROAR.

96
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:13,000
 And if we would have used an approximation, so we would have gone from here to there.

97
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 We would have our tax rate, we would have had 35% instead of the 36%.

98
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 So here in this case you could argue that this is a lot of work for a very close internal rate of return difference.

99
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
 Before and after tax replacement study.

100
